`tessera bench compare` is the head-to-head behind the Parquet/HDF5 pitch (#388). Every number here
varies by machine and run, so this walkthrough asserts the STRUCTURE — that each format appears at
both its default and a tuned setting, that the settings are printed, that the cache state and N are
stated, and that the honesty scaffolding (the caveat, the per-row claims, the integrity wording) is
present. The numbers themselves are wildcarded.

A tiny fixture keeps it fast; the real run uses the defaults.

```
$ tessera bench compare --dataset volume --vol-n 64 --iters 1
tessera bench compare — .tsra vs HDF5 vs Parquet (#388)
  data     volume 64^3 int16 = 512.0 KiB raw
  machine [..]
  versions [..]
  method   median of 1 runs, spread = [min..max]; EVERY timed read is verified against
           the source data first (full, projected and ROI, per format and setting)
  warm-up  none beyond the correctness pass: the verification read precedes the warm
           timings, so warm rows start with the file in page cache; cold rows evict first
  cache    warm only (pass --cold to add best-effort cold-cache rows)
  note     three formats: .tsra, HDF5 and Parquet (#388)
  fixtures table (periodic)   = the original #388 fixture: f32 columns of period 7 and 5.
           ADVERSARIAL for value-distribution codecs — deflate's LZ77 locks onto the
           repeating byte block; Pco/dict/bitpacking model values, not repetition (#493).
           table (continuous) = shaped after REAL DUPLET listmode: a coarse ms clock
           (~99.6% zero deltas) + continuous energies. Both are reported, always: showing
           only one of them would be choosing the answer.
  CAVEAT   the VOLUME is a smooth gradient (verbatim from the #143 harness, for
           comparability), and the PERIODIC table is adversarial by construction. Both
           are far more compressible than a real acquisition, so read every size row as
           a RATIO BETWEEN FORMATS on identical input, not as a compression ratio to
           expect clinically. The CONTINUOUS table is the one shaped after real listmode.

format             settings                                                 data    op             cache       size          median [min..max]
Tessera (.tsra)    pcodec (default), 64^3 chunks                            volume  size           -       10.[..]
Tessera (.tsra)    pcodec (default), 64^3 chunks                            volume  write+seal     warm[..]
Tessera (.tsra)    pcodec (default), 64^3 chunks                            volume  read full      warm[..]
Tessera (.tsra)    pcodec (default), 64^3 chunks                            volume  read ROI       warm[..]
Tessera (.tsra)    pcodec (default), 64^3 chunks                            volume  verify         warm[..]
Tessera (.tsra)    zstd (tuned), 64^3 chunks                                volume  size           -        9.[..]
Tessera (.tsra)    zstd (tuned), 64^3 chunks                                volume  write+seal     warm[..]
Tessera (.tsra)    zstd (tuned), 64^3 chunks                                volume  read full      warm[..]
Tessera (.tsra)    zstd (tuned), 64^3 chunks                                volume  read ROI       warm[..]
Tessera (.tsra)    zstd (tuned), 64^3 chunks                                volume  verify         warm[..]
HDF5 (hdf5-metno)  default: contiguous, uncompressed                        volume  size           -      514.[..]
HDF5 (hdf5-metno)  default: contiguous, uncompressed                        volume  write+seal     warm[..]
HDF5 (hdf5-metno)  default: contiguous, uncompressed                        volume  read full      warm[..]
HDF5 (hdf5-metno)  default: contiguous, uncompressed                        volume  read ROI       warm[..]
HDF5 (hdf5-metno)  default: contiguous, uncompressed                        volume  verify         -[..]
HDF5 (hdf5-metno)  tuned: chunked 64^3 (= tessera's), shuffle+gzip-4        volume  size           -       15.[..]
HDF5 (hdf5-metno)  tuned: chunked 64^3 (= tessera's), shuffle+gzip-4        volume  write+seal     warm[..]
HDF5 (hdf5-metno)  tuned: chunked 64^3 (= tessera's), shuffle+gzip-4        volume  read full      warm[..]
HDF5 (hdf5-metno)  tuned: chunked 64^3 (= tessera's), shuffle+gzip-4        volume  read ROI       warm[..]
HDF5 (hdf5-metno)  tuned: chunked 64^3 (= tessera's), shuffle+gzip-4        volume  verify         -[..]
HDF5 (hdf5-metno)  tuned: chunked 64^3 (= tessera's), shuffle+gzip-4, fletcher32 volume  size           -[..]
HDF5 (hdf5-metno)  tuned: chunked 64^3 (= tessera's), shuffle+gzip-4, fletcher32 volume  write+seal     warm[..]
HDF5 (hdf5-metno)  tuned: chunked 64^3 (= tessera's), shuffle+gzip-4, fletcher32 volume  read full      warm[..]
HDF5 (hdf5-metno)  tuned: chunked 64^3 (= tessera's), shuffle+gzip-4, fletcher32 volume  read ROI       warm[..]
HDF5 (hdf5-metno)  tuned: chunked 64^3 (= tessera's), shuffle+gzip-4, fletcher32 volume  verify         warm[..]

What each row supports:
  size           smaller on disk than the alternatives
  write+seal     the cost of sealing (tessera pays, the others do not)
  read full      baseline full materialise
  read ROI       cubic chunks -> cheap ROI / orthogonal access
  verify         tamper-evident: re-derives every digest against the sealed manifest

Integrity is not one thing — see the module docs for the long form:
  fletcher32 detects CORRUPTION on the chunks you read. It is unkeyed and covers no metadata.
  tessera verify re-derives every block digest against the sealed manifest, whose hash also
  covers metadata + provenance, and which a signature can bind to a signer. Both catch a
  flipped bit; only one answers 'is this the artifact that was sealed, and by whom'.

```

`--format json` is the CI shape. The metadata block states the method so a consumer never has to
guess how the numbers were produced; `rows` is elided here because its contents are timings.

```
$ tessera bench compare --dataset table --rows 5000 --iters 1 --format json
{
  "benchmark": "tessera bench compare",
  "issue": 388,
  "iters": 1,
  "statistic": "median with [min..max] spread",
  "cold_cache": false,
  "cold_method": "n/a",
  "cold_eviction": "n/a",
  "machine": {
    "cpu": "[..]",
    "cores": [..],
    "ram": "[..]",
    "bench_dir_fs": "[..]",
    "kernel": "[..]"
  },
  "versions": {
    "tessera": "[..]",
    "libhdf5": "[..]"
  },
  "verification": "every timed read (full, projected, ROI) is compared against the source data, per format and setting, before its timings count",
  "volume_n": null,
  "table_rows": 5000,
  "caveat": "the VOLUME is a smooth gradient (verbatim from the #143 harness) and the PERIODIC table is adversarial by construction; both are far more compressible than a real acquisition, so size rows are a ratio BETWEEN formats on identical input, not a compression ratio to expect clinically. The CONTINUOUS table is the one shaped after real listmode",
  "parquet": "included (parquet-rs); its writer emits no page CRC, so the integrity row is absent by fact, not omission",
  "rows": [
...
  ]
}

```
